namespace Jazz2 {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page asset-packer Asset packing
@brief Offline data conversion tool of Jazz² Resurrection

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

AssetPacker (@cpp "Sources/Utilities/AssetPacker" @ce) is a standalone command-line tool that converts
the original Jazz Jackrabbit 2 data into the layout a given platform loads. The game does the same
conversion on its first run @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GameEventHandler::RefreshCache`, which forwards to
@ref Jazz2::Compatibility::AssetConverter) @m_endspan; the tool prepares the data <b>ahead of time</b>
for platforms that cannot convert it themselves (the consoles and the web build) and for build pipelines.

It also packs and unpacks bitmap fonts (@ref Jazz2::UI::FontFormat), makes indexed font images
editable, and re-encodes `.j2v` cinematics into the game's own @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat.

@section asset-packer-building Building the tool

-   Built by default with the desktop CMake build (`NCINE_BUILD_ASSET_PACKER`, default @cpp ON @ce),
    skipped for cross-compiled targets except the web version below. It links only the base layer and
    the converters --- no renderer, window backend or networking.
-   After a regular @ref building "desktop build" the binary is
    @cpp "build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker" @ce. `NCINE_BUILD_GAME=OFF` builds only the
    offline tools.
-   With Emscripten it builds the web version: a WebAssembly module with the repository's `Content`
    baked in and a page that swaps the content of a Nintendo 64, Dreamcast, PlayStation 2 or GameCube
    image in the browser, using the user's own copy of the original game
    (`swap-content --source=`, see @ref asset-packer-swap-content):

@code{.sh}
emcmake cmake -S . -B build/assetpacker-web -G Ninja -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -D NCINE_BUILD_GAME=OFF
cmake --build build/assetpacker-web
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The build directory's `Utilities/AssetPacker` then holds the whole site; deployment is described in
@cpp "Sources/Utilities/AssetPacker/Web/README.md" @ce. The Emscripten workflow publishes it as the
`Jazz2_AssetPacker_Web` artifact.

@section asset-packer-usage Command-line reference

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker [<command>] [<source>] <target> [options]
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Without a command, `convert` is assumed.

-   `convert [<source directory>] <target directory>` --- Converts the original game data
    -   `<source directory>` --- The original game files (`Anims.j2a`, `*.j2l`, `*.j2t`, ...), or a
        game installation keeping them in a `Source` subdirectory, in which case its `Content` is
        copied too
    -   `<target directory>` --- Output directory, created if needed
    -   `--source=<dir>` --- Same as `<source directory>`; giving both is an error
    -   `--content=<dir>` --- The game's own content (fonts, `Animations`, `Metadata`, translations ---
        the repository's `Content`). Overrides a `Content` found beside the originals; needed for a
        self-contained console or web tree when the two are not kept together
    -   `--target=<profile>` --- `desktop` (default) \| `console` \| `dreamcast` \| `wii` \|
        `gamecube` \| `psp` \| `ps2` \| `n64` \| `emscripten` (or `web`)
    -   `--n64-tools=<dir>` --- libdragon's host tools (the toolchain prefix or its `bin`) for
        `--target=n64`; defaults to `$N64_INST`, falls back to the built-in encoders, and `builtin`
        forces them. See @ref asset-packer-n64
    -   `--video-downscale=N` --- Downscale cinematics by N (1--4, 1 keeps the original size).
        Defaults to 2 for `ps2` and 1 elsewhere. See @ref asset-packer-cinematics
    -   `--originals-only` --- Convert only the episodes and levels the original game shipped
    -   `--shareware-only` --- Convert only what the Shareware Demo shipped
        @m_span{m-text m-dim} (implies `--originals-only`) @m_endspan
    -   `--all-videos` --- Deploy every cinematic found, not just the two the game plays
    -   `--skip-non-episode-levels` --- Convert only levels that belong to an episode
-   `pack-font <source .png> <target .font>` --- Packs a grid image and its character list (read
    from `<source .png>.json`, or from the binary `<source .png>.font` if there is no JSON)
-   `unpack-font <source .font> <target .png>` --- Unpacks a font into a grid image plus
    `<target .png>.json` (the one to edit) and the binary `<target .png>.font`
-   `apply-palette <source .png> <target .png>` --- Replaces palette indices with their colors, for
    editing
-   `to-indices <source .png> <target .png>` --- Resolves an edited image back to the nearest palette
    indices
-   `recompress-video <source .j2v> <target .j2v> [--video-downscale=N] [--target=<profile>]` ---
    Re-encodes one cinematic; N defaults to 1 or to the profile's value, and the profile also decides
    LZ4 frames (see @ref asset-packer-cinematics-format)
-   `convert-music <source .j2b> <target .xm>` --- Translates a Galaxy Music System module into a
    FastTracker II module (the first half of what `--target=n64` does to music)
-   `swap-content <source image> [<target image>] --content=<dir>` --- Replaces the `Content` of a
    built Dreamcast `.cdi`, PlayStation 2 `.iso`, GameCube `.iso` or Nintendo 64 `.z64`, keeping its
    bootstrap and executable. Without a target the image is rewritten in place
-   `swap-content <source image> [<target image>] --source=<dir> [--content=<dir>] [options]` ---
    The same, converting the original files first for the image's console: `.z64` is `n64`, a
    DiscJuggler `.cdi` `dreamcast`, an image with a GameCube disc header `gamecube`, anything else
    `ps2`, unless `--target=` says otherwise. `--content=` and the `convert` options apply as for
    `convert`
-   `create-image <executable .dol> <target .iso> --content=<dir> --apploader=<file> [--target=gamecube]
    [--game-id=<id>] [--title=<name>]` --- Makes a bootable GameCube disc image (see
    @ref asset-packer-gamecube). `--game-id=` is the six-character game + maker code (default
    `GJJEJR`), `--title=` the name in the disc header
-   `verify-formats <directory> [--sweep-truncated]` --- Reads every level and tile set in the game's own
    format under the directory (recursively) and writes it back into memory, which has to give the same
    file byte for byte, or, for a file written by another tool, the same content once read back. Original
    JJ2 files are skipped. `--sweep-truncated` also reads every file cut short. It checks @ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelReader, @ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelWriter and
    @ref Jazz2::Formats::TileSetFile against real data, which the editors rely on when they save a file
    they opened (@ref Jazz2::AssetPacker::FormatVerifier). See @ref asset-packer-formats
-   `level-to-text <source .j2l> <target .txt>` --- Writes a level of the game's own format as text, one
    thing on a line, so versions of it can be compared and merged by the usual tools. The text is checked
    to give the same level again before it's written. See @ref asset-packer-formats-text
-   `text-to-level <source .txt> <target .j2l>` --- Makes the level of such a text again, the same file
    byte for byte as the level it came from
-   `fuzz-formats <directory> [<findings directory>] [--iterations=N] [--seed=N] [--timeout=seconds]
    [--memory-limit=MB] [--focus-level-texts]` --- Attacks the readers with seeded random mutations of the
    levels and tile sets in the game's own format under the directory. See @ref asset-packer-formats-fuzz

Progress and warnings go to stdout, errors to stderr; the exit code is non-zero on failure.

@section asset-packer-convert Converting the game data

A conversion has <b>two inputs</b>: the original game files and the game's own `Content`. A game
installation keeps both together (originals in `Source`, beside `Content` and `Cache`); when the source
is an installation and the target is not `desktop`, its `Content` is copied as well. From a repository
checkout, name them separately:

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker ./build/ConsoleContent --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=console
@endcode

-   `Anims.j2a` (or the shareware `AnimsSw.j2a`) is required. Sprites and sounds go into a package
    (`Source.pak` for `desktop`, `Prebaked.pak` otherwise); levels, tilesets, episodes and music follow
    via @ref Jazz2::Compatibility::AssetConverter.
-   `Plus.j2d` of a JJ2+ installation, if it's beside the levels, gives a level made for JJ2+ the textures
    its textured layers name (`TEXTURE::Texture`), stored in the converted level. Without it, such a layer
    gets a texture made up of smooth noise in the same colors, so a reflection still ripples.
-   `--source=` and the positional source resolve the same way; with `--source=` a lone positional
    argument is the target.
-   `--content=` wins over a `Content` found beside the originals.

@subsection asset-packer-convert-profiles Target profiles

Profile     | Output
----------- | ------
`desktop`   | A `Cache` subdirectory plus the `Source.idx` index the game checks on startup (see @ref asset-packer-pitfalls). No music (the game reads its `Content/Music`) and no cinematics (read from the originals)
`console`, `dreamcast`, `wii`, `gamecube`, `psp`, `ps2` | A staged `Content` tree written directly into the target, with `Prebaked.pak` and no index. The target name only decides the cinematics and LZ4 images
`emscripten` | The same kind of prepared tree for the web build
`n64`       | A console tree with sound, music and cinematics in libdragon's formats, see @ref asset-packer-n64

Non-desktop trees:

-   The name `Prebaked.pak` marks the tree as converted, so consoles that can convert (PSP, Wii,
    PlayStation 3) skip the conversion --- see @ref Jazz2::ContentResolver::IsContentPrebaked(). No
    index is written because a prepared tree is never rewritten.
-   Converted levels carry their own version (@ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelFile) and the game reads only its own,
    so a tree from an older tool must be converted again --- see the warning in
    @ref consoles-common-content.
-   The game's own `Animations` and `Metadata` go <b>inside</b> `Prebaked.pak`; translations, levels,
    tilesets, music and cinematics stay loose files. Converted files are written first, so on a path
    present in both, the asset from the original data wins.
-   A hand-made multi-frame sprite sheet in `Content/Animations` wider or taller than 512 pixels is
    re-laid out @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref Jazz2::AssetPacker::SpriteRepacker) @m_endspan: frames are
    trimmed and packed so none crosses the PSP's 512-pixel texture page line. Smaller grids are copied
    as they are, since some are sampled as a whole texture.
-   `.po` files in `Translations` are left out; the game loads only `.mo`.

@subsection asset-packer-convert-filters Level filters

-   `--originals-only` keeps the original episodes plus every level reachable from them;
    `--shareware-only` narrows that to the Shareware Demo; `--skip-non-episode-levels` drops levels
    that belong to no episode.
-   Skipped levels are listed by name --- the list of original levels is maintained by hand, so check
    it for levels that should have been kept.
-   Music: a track is kept if a surviving level uses it, plus `menu`, `bonus2`, `bonus3` (main menu),
    `intro` and `ending` (cinematics), which no `.j2l` names.

@section asset-packer-cinematics Cinematics

The game plays only `Intro` and `Ending`; `Logo` is deployed only with `--all-videos`. Files go to a
`Cinematics` subdirectory under lowercase names.

Target          | Cinematics
--------------- | ----------
`desktop`       | Nothing --- the game reads the originals in place
`dreamcast`, `gamecube`, `ps2`, or any profile with `--video-downscale` above 1 | Re-encoded into @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat
Everything else | Copied unchanged

Inflating the original format is too slow on the Dreamcast and PlayStation 2, and on the GameCube its
four interleaved streams make disc reads too slow; the re-encoded file is read sequentially. The
Dreamcast and GameCube keep 640×480; the PS2 defaults to `--video-downscale=2` (320×240, drawn smoothly
upscaled) to keep disc reads small. The player picks its runtime downscale from the actual frame width,
so a pre-downscaled file is not halved again.

@subsection asset-packer-cinematics-format The .j2v recompression

The original `.j2v` (`CineFeed` signature) is 640×480 in four interleaved zlib streams.
@ref Jazz2::Compatibility::J2vRecompressor decodes it exactly as the player does, optionally downscales
it, and re-encodes it into @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat. The player detects either format by its signature.

-   <b>At the original size</b> frames are taken over exactly, indices and palettes alike.
-   <b>Downscaled</b>, each pixel is the area average in linear light, and every stretch of frames sharing
    one original palette gets a new palette (median cut refined by k-means).

The container: the game's usual signature/type/version fields, then width, height, frame delay, frame
count, pixel format, codec and an extension area older players skip, then one size-prefixed payload per
frame. A frame is a flag byte @m_span{m-text m-dim} (bit 0 --- a 256-entry BGRA palette follows)
@m_endspan and commands up to an end-of-frame marker:

-   <b>literal</b> --- bytes stored as they are
-   <b>run</b> --- one repeated byte
-   <b>skip</b> --- keep the previous frame's pixels

Each command has a single-byte short form and a long form with a 16-bit count; opcode ranges are on
@ref Jazz2::VideoFormat. Decoding is only @cpp memcpy @ce and @cpp memset @ce over a reused frame buffer.

-   The re-encoded file is substantially larger than the zlib original, so re-encode only where the
    platform needs it (the per-target defaults already do). Use `recompress-video` to experiment.
-   `dreamcast`, `gamecube` and `ps2` also compress every frame payload as an LZ4HC block (codec
    @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat::CodecDeltaRleLz4), because the plain stream exceeds what their drives
    deliver. A player without LZ4 refuses such a file, so `swap-content` writes LZ4 frames only onto a
    disc whose cinematics already have them (see @ref asset-packer-swap-content).

@section asset-packer-fonts Font packing

A font is authored as a grid image (one equal cell per character) plus a description of the cell size,
character set and advances. The game loads a single `.font` file (version 2,
@ref Jazz2::UI::FontFormat) with every glyph trimmed, packed and stored as palette indices.

The description exists as `<image>.png.json` (the one to edit) and the binary `<image>.png.font` (same
fields, non-ASCII characters in UTF-8). `unpack-font` writes both; `pack-font` uses the JSON if present,
otherwise the binary, and prints which one it used.

`pack-font` does:

-   <b>Trimming</b> --- Each glyph is reduced to its inked bounding box; the rest of the cell becomes its
    bearing, so it draws exactly where the cell did. A glyph with no inked pixels (a space) has zero size.
-   <b>Index normalization</b> --- Every pixel is either a palette index or index 0 (nothing). Ambiguous
    pixels (transparent in one channel only, partially covered) are resolved, with a warning giving the
    count.
-   <b>Packing</b> --- As for converted sprite sheets
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref Jazz2::Compatibility::JJ2Anims::PackRectangles()) @m_endspan, with a
    @cpp FontFormat::GlyphMargin @ce (1 px) margin around each glyph. The chosen atlas minimizes the
    power-of-two-padded area, then stays within one 512×512 page if possible, then minimizes the exact
    area; it is stored at its exact size. No glyph crosses a multiple of 512 on either axis (PSP texture
    pages).
-   <b>Encoding</b> --- After the identifying bytes (signature, file type, version, flags, compressed
    size), one deflate block holds the atlas size, line height, base spacing, character counts, a 9-byte
    glyph record per character @m_span{m-text m-dim} (position, size, bearings, advance; plus a 32-bit
    codepoint for non-ASCII) @m_endspan, and the atlas as palette indices in the QOI-based image encoding.

Limits (exceeding any is an error):

Item                          | Maximum
----------------------------- | -------
Atlas                         | 1024×1024
Glyph size                    | 255 px in either direction
Bearing                       | 127
Characters in the ASCII range | 255
Characters outside it         | 65535

`unpack-font` places glyphs back into cells at their bearings, so repacking gives the same font. The
unpacked atlas holds palette indices (it looks like near-black noise): run `apply-palette` before
editing and `to-indices` after, which warns when a color has no exact match. PNG images are read and
written by @ref nCine::PngCodec, the codec the game and the level editor share, which needs no renderer;
the tool defines `WITH_PNG_ENCODER` for its encoder, which the game without the level editor leaves out.

@subsection asset-packer-fonts-json The JSON description

@code{.json}
{
    "CellWidth": 15,
    "CellHeight": 20,
    "Columns": 19,
    "LineHeight": 20,
    "BaseSpacing": -2,
    "AsciiFirst": 32,
    "AsciiCount": 95,
    "Characters": [
        { "Char": " ", "Advance": 5 },
        { "Char": "!", "Advance": 4 },
        { "Char": "\"", "Advance": 6 },
        { "Char": "é", "Advance": 8 },
        { "Codepoint": 120071, "Advance": 11 },
        { "Fallback": true, "Advance": 8 }
    ]
}
@endcode

Field                                 | Meaning
------------------------------------- | -------
`CellWidth`, `CellHeight`, `Columns`  | Required. Cell size (up to 65535) and cells per row (up to 255)
`LineHeight`                          | Distance between lines; defaults to the cell height
`BaseSpacing`                         | Pixels added between characters, negative to tighten (signed 16-bit); default 0
`AsciiFirst`, `AsciiCount`            | The contiguous directly-indexed run; derived from the leading run of consecutive characters if omitted. The first `AsciiCount` entries must be exactly that run, it must end by `U+00FF`, and the game keeps only the part below `U+0080`
`Characters`                          | Required, at least one entry, <b>in the order of the cells in the image</b>. `Char` gives the character, `Codepoint` a number, `Fallback` marks the single glyph drawn for missing characters (same as @cpp "Codepoint": 0 @ce). `Advance` (required, up to 255) is the pen movement; size and bearings are measured from the image

A field out of range, missing or of the wrong type is an error naming it (and the list position), as is a
list not starting with its claimed ASCII range --- which also catches a duplicate inside it:

@code{.shell-session}
Error: "font_small.png.json" has to list the ASCII range first, one character after another
from U+0020, but the character at position 34 is U+0041 where U+0042 was expected
@endcode

A codepoint listed twice outside the range, or a character below `U+0080` left out of it, is only a
warning, but the game never draws that glyph:

@code{.shell-session}
Warning: "font_small.png.json" lists U+0104 more than once
@endcode

Comments, trailing commas and a byte order mark are tolerated; the tool writes UTF-8 without a BOM.

@section asset-packer-n64 Preparing content for the Nintendo 64

`--target=n64` converts sound, music and cinematics into libdragon's formats, so the console can play
music and everything fits a 64 MB cartridge. If libdragon's host tools are available it runs
`audioconv64`, and `videoconv64` with <b>ffmpeg</b> and <b>ffprobe</b> on the `PATH`:

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker convert ./build/ConsoleContentN64 --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content \
    --target=n64 --n64-tools=<libdragon toolchain prefix>
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Otherwise (or with `--n64-tools=builtin`) it uses built-in encoders, the only option in the web
version: libdragon's own codecs vendored in `Sources/Dependencies` (VADPCM, ULC, libxm, driven by
`Wav64Writer` and `Xm64Converter`) and its own `Mpeg1Encoder`. The output plays the same --- sound effects
and pre-rendered music are byte-identical to `audioconv64`'s. The built-in encoders compile only with
GCC, Clang and Emscripten, so an MSVC build of the tool needs libdragon's tools for this profile.

Differences from a `console` tree:

-   <b>Sound effects</b> --- written beside the package as one VADPCM `.wav64` each
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Animations/<path>.wav64`) @m_endspan, streamed from the cartridge. VADPCM is
    used rather than raw PCM, which costs the mixer more per frame. The originals are not kept: a sound
    without its `.wav64` is silent.
-   <b>Music</b> --- `.j2b` modules are translated to FastTracker II modules (as `convert-music`) and then
    to `.xm64`. Other formats are pre-rendered through libopenmpt into a looping `.wav64`, which needs a
    packer built with libopenmpt.
-   <b>Cinematics</b> --- MPEG-1 video (`<name>.m1v`, 320×240, 24 fps) with a `.wav64` soundtrack mixing
    music and sound effects. Without `videoconv64` or ffmpeg they are re-encoded the `console` way at half
    size.
-   Sprite sheets and tilesets are written in LZ4 (see @ref asset-packer-lz4). The full game comes to
    about 38 MB.

@section asset-packer-lz4 LZ4 sprite sheets and tilesets

`--target=n64`, `dreamcast`, `ps2` and `gamecube` write every sprite sheet and tileset as LZ4HC blocks of
raw pixels; other targets keep the game's own format. These four builds decode it with `NCINE_WITH_LZ4`
(on by default for exactly them).

-   A <b>tileset</b> gets one block per 32-row band (one row of tiles), the unit the game builds its atlas
    in.
-   A <b>sprite sheet</b> is one block, stored in the package without further compression.
-   Hand-made sheets from `Content` are converted too; fonts and episode images keep the game's format.

Both headers carry `JJ2Anims::ImageContentLz4Flag`, so a build without LZ4 reports the file instead of
drawing garbage --- rebuild the tree for that target, or the target with `NCINE_WITH_LZ4`.

@section asset-packer-swap-content Replacing the content of a disc image

The Dreamcast, PlayStation 2 and GameCube boot from read-only discs whose content is baked in at build
time @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-dreamcast-content, @ref consoles-ps2-content and
@ref consoles-gamecube-disc) @m_endspan. `swap-content` opens a finished image (or Nintendo 64 ROM),
keeps everything except `Content`, and writes it back around a new one --- no console toolchain needed.

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker convert ./build/DreamcastContent --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=dreamcast
AssetPacker swap-content ./jazz2.cdi --content=./build/DreamcastContent

AssetPacker convert ./build/Ps2Content --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=ps2
AssetPacker swap-content ./jazz2.iso --content=./build/Ps2Content

AssetPacker convert ./build/ConsoleContentN64 --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=n64
AssetPacker swap-content ./jazz2.z64 --content=./build/ConsoleContentN64

AssetPacker convert ./build/ConsoleContentGameCube --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=gamecube
AssetPacker swap-content ./Jazz2.iso --content=./build/ConsoleContentGameCube
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

With `--source=` both steps are one (this is what the web version runs):

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker swap-content ./jazz2.z64 ./jazz2-new.z64 --source=<original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content
@endcode

-   The `--content=` directory becomes `Content` whatever its name, so it must be a conversion for that
    console's target.
-   With `--source=`, LZ4 images (@ref asset-packer-lz4) and LZ4 cinematic frames
    (@ref asset-packer-cinematics-format) are written only if the content already on the disc has them,
    since older game builds draw garbage from LZ4 images or refuse LZ4 videos. GameCube discs and
    Nintendo 64 cartridges always get LZ4 images.
-   Without a target image the source is rewritten in place via a `.tmp` beside it, which replaces it only
    once fully written.

What is kept unchanged:

Image                | Kept
-------------------- | ----
Dreamcast `.cdi`     | `IP.BIN` (32 KB: bootstrap, licence screen, serial number, title) and `1ST_READ.BIN`, still scrambled
PlayStation 2 `.iso` | `SYSTEM.CNF`, the executable it names, the `.IRX` modules
GameCube `.iso`      | Disc header and apploader (first 32 KB) and `Jazz2.dol` --- which moves, and the header's pointer to it is updated
Nintendo 64 `.z64`   | Header (save type, accessories), bootcode, executable and its symbol table

On a Nintendo 64 ROM the content is the DragonFS image at the end, found via the table of contents
`n64tool` writes after the bootcode. It is rebuilt like `mkdfs` does (the same tree gives an identical ROM
except the table's cookie) and staged like the build does @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(`cmake/n64_stage_content.cmake`: original music formats and cinematics that have a video are left out,
`Source.pak` becomes `Prebaked.pak`) @m_endspan. A ROM that would exceed 64 MB is refused.

@subsection asset-packer-swap-content-how What it actually does

The file system is generated from scratch (@ref Jazz2::AssetPacker::Iso9660Builder), since any size change
moves every following file; only the container is copied byte for byte. For a DiscJuggler `.cdi` that is
the first session's audio track, the pregap, the track layout and the trailing descriptor; a plain `.iso`
is just the data track of 2048-byte sectors. The type is read from the file's last eight bytes.

The volume matches what `mkdcdisc` writes:

-   <b>Two hierarchies</b> --- plain ISO 9660 (where the Dreamcast bootstrap finds `1ST_READ.BIN`, with
    upper-case restricted names) plus Joliet with the real names, which KallistiOS uses whenever present
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`fs_iso9660.c`) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Files at the outer edge</b> --- file data is placed at the end of the volume for faster reads
    (`mkdcdisc`'s default, `-N` turns it off), which is why the image is far larger than its content.
-   <b>Mode 2 Form 1 sectors</b> for a `.cdi` (2336 bytes stored per sector), with the error detection code
    and both Reed-Solomon parities generated --- wrong parity means read errors. A plain `.iso` stores 2048
    bytes per sector and needs none of it.

A `.cdi` keeps its length when the new content fits; otherwise the track grows and the descriptor's three
length fields and the disc length are adjusted. A disc exceeding an 80-minute CD is still written, with a
warning. A plain `.iso` is always sized to its content.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par The PlayStation 2 disc gains a Joliet hierarchy
ps2sdk's `cdfs.irx` ignores the <em>Rock Ridge</em> names `mkisofs` writes and reads Joliet if present,
otherwise the plain names --- so the build passes `-J`, and names with a space or dash
(`xmas 1 feaw.j2t`, `christmas-x-core_jj2.it`) stay reachable. Images from `swap-content` always have
Joliet.

@section asset-packer-gamecube The GameCube disc image

`create-image` writes the GameCube disc image; the GameCube build runs it when given a host build of the
tool (`GAMECUBE_ASSETPACKER_EXECUTABLE`, see @ref consoles-gamecube-disc). It is an ordinary ISO 9660
volume (@ref Jazz2::AssetPacker::Iso9660Builder) whose first 32 KB (the system area) hold:

-   the <b>disc header</b> (`boot.bin`): game ID, GameCube magic word, title and executable offset,
-   <b>`bi2.bin`</b>: the assumed memory size and the region, which the boot ROM checks,
-   the <b>apploader</b>, which loads the executable. Nintendo's cannot be redistributed and devkitPro
    ships none, so the GameCube target builds a minimal one from
    @cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Ogc/GameCubeApploader.c" @ce.

The executable and `Content` are ordinary files with Joliet names; the game reads them through ISO 9660
(see `OgcDvd.h`). The volume is followed by 32 KB of zeros, because a read past the image end is an error
in Dolphin. There is no GameCube file system table, so tools that list a disc by it show no files.

@section asset-packer-formats Checking the level and tile set formats

Levels and tile sets in the game's own format (@ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelFile, @ref Jazz2::Formats::TileSetFile) are
read by the game, the level editor and the converter alike, and can come from anywhere --- custom levels, servers,
packages. Three commands check the readers and writers against real files; see also @ref editor-formats.

@subsection asset-packer-formats-verify Verifying files

`verify-formats <directory> [--sweep-truncated]` goes through every native level and tile set under the directory:

-   Each is read whole and written back into memory: byte for byte the same file, or, for a file written by another
    tool, the same content once read back again.
-   With `--sweep-truncated`, each is also read cut short at many places --- at every byte of its first 32, at 40
    places spread over the rest, and at each of its last 4 bytes. A cut level must never be read as anything but the
    whole level (a cut that takes only the end of the compressed body can leave all of it readable), a tile set cut
    before its image content must be refused. That reads every file 76 more times, which makes the whole check many
    times slower, so it's left for when the readers themselves change.
-   Levels are checked for what the reader can't check on its own, how the records refer to each other: a default or
    extra tile set path the game wouldn't look up (@ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelFile::IsSafeTileSetPath()), an embedded
    tile set that can't be read or has fewer tiles than the level takes, tiles and animation frames beyond the tiles of
    the level, overridden tiles that aren't of a tile set, and off-grid events far outside of the level. These are
    warnings --- the game copes with them --- and don't fail the check.

The exit code is non-zero if a file differs, can't be read, or (with `--sweep-truncated`) passes for something else
when cut.

@subsection asset-packer-formats-text Levels as text

`level-to-text <source .j2l> <target .txt>` writes a level as text (@ref Jazz2::AssetPacker::LevelText), and
`text-to-level <source .txt> <target .j2l>` makes the level of it again --- the same file byte for byte, as
@ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelWriter writes it. `level-to-text` checks that before it writes the text. Levels kept as text
can be compared line by line and merged by the usual tools of version control.

-   The text starts with a `format` line, one thing follows on a line, lines starting with `#` are comments.
-   Strings are quoted, with C escapes for `"`, `\` and the control characters. Floating-point values are written
    so they're read back exactly, or as `bits:XXXXXXXX` if they aren't numbers.
-   A tile is its ID, followed by `:` and its flags in hexadecimal if it has any. Only the events of the grid that
    aren't empty are written, each with its position.
-   Binary data --- the tile sets a level stores in itself, the payload of a chunk --- is written as Base64 lines up to
    `end`.

@subsection asset-packer-formats-fuzz Fuzzing the readers

`fuzz-formats <directory> [<findings directory>]` attacks the readers with seeded random mutations of the native levels
and tile sets under the directory (@ref Jazz2::AssetPacker::FormatVerifier::FuzzDirectory()): of the file itself, of
its decompressed content (inflated, mutated and deflated again, so the readers are attacked rather than the framing of
the compression), of the chunks and records of a level, and of the tile sets it embeds. Every reader runs on each
input, and what a reader accepts has to survive being written and read again. Every fourth level is also tried with a
chunk of translated texts (@ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelTextsChunk), which converted levels don't have.

An input that crashes, fails an assertion, hangs, asks for too much memory or doesn't survive is saved into the
findings directory (@cpp "FuzzFindings" @ce by default) as `<reason>-<index>-<file>`; a crash, a hang or too much
memory also ends the run. Given a single file instead of a directory, the command only runs it through the readers
once, to reproduce a finding.

Option | Meaning
------ | -------
`--iterations=N` | Number of inputs to try, 10000 by default
`--seed=N` | Seed of the mutations, 1 by default; the same seed tries the same inputs again
`--timeout=seconds` | How long a single input may take before it counts as a hang, 30 by default
`--memory-limit=MB` | Memory the process may use before an input counts as asking for too much, 3072 by default, at least 64 (Windows only)
`--focus-level-texts` | Only levels, each given a chunk of translated texts, and that chunk attacked more

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker fuzz-formats ./Cache --iterations=100000 --seed=7
AssetPacker fuzz-formats ./FuzzFindings/crash-1234-castle1.j2l
@endcode

@section asset-packer-platforms Platform notes

Where each console expects its tree is described in @ref consoles-common-content "Building for consoles".

Platform           | Profile                  | Notes
------------------ | ------------------------ | -----
Desktop            | `desktop`                | Optional --- the game converts on first run. Useful for build pipelines or pre-downscaling cinematics
Dreamcast, GameCube | `dreamcast`, `gamecube` | <b>Required</b> (no conversion compiled in). Re-encoded cinematics, LZ4 images. Baked into a disc image, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-content and @ref consoles-gamecube-disc; replace later with @ref asset-packer-swap-content
PSP, Wii           | `psp`, `wii`             | Could convert an installation in `Source`, but slowly, so a prepared tree is used. Original cinematics copied
PlayStation 2      | `ps2`                    | <b>Required</b>. Cinematics re-encoded at 320×240, LZ4 images; a `console` tree plays, but slower. Baked into an ISO (@ref consoles-ps2-content), or run from an SD card in an MX4SIO adapter (@ref consoles-ps2-storage)
Nintendo 64        | `n64`                    | See @ref asset-packer-n64; a `console` tree boots without music. Baked into the ROM (@ref consoles-n64-content)
PlayStation 3      | `console`                | Could convert, but slowly
PS Vita, Switch    | none                     | Convert on first run; the tool only skips the wait
Web                | `emscripten`             | Typically with `--shareware-only` for the public demo

Packed `.font` files are loaded on all platforms; consoles gain the most (one index per pixel and less
power-of-two padding).

@section asset-packer-pitfalls Pitfalls

-   <b>`Source.idx` must match the game exactly.</b> It is compared field for field with what
    `GameEventHandler::WriteCacheDescriptor` writes (signature, file type, cache version, flags,
    `Anims.j2a` modification time, event count, build version); any mismatch makes the game silently
    reconvert everything. Use a tool built from the same sources as the game.
-   <b>Never add an index to a console or web tree</b>, and <b>never rename `Prebaked.pak`</b> --- the game
    would look for original files it does not have and show a menu with nothing to play.
-   <b>Missing `Anims.j2a`</b> (or `AnimsSw.j2a`) at the top of the source or in its `Source` subdirectory
    makes the tool refuse to run.
-   <b>A non-desktop tree needs the game's own content</b> --- a `Content` beside the originals or
    `--content=`. Without it the conversion still succeeds but the game cannot draw its menu (no fonts,
    `Metadata`, UI graphics or translations); the tool warns. A run that got it prints `Copying "…"`.
-   <b>`swap-content` reads only what the game's builds produce</b>: a plain `.iso`, a DiscJuggler `.cdi`
    with 2048 or 2336 bytes per sector (`mkdcdisc`), and a `.z64` ending with its DragonFS image. A
    2352-byte-sector `.cdi` is refused, a byte-swapped `.v64`/`.n64` must be converted to `.z64` first,
    and other containers @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`.mds`, `.nrg`, `.gdi`) @m_endspan are not supported.
-   <b>`.xm64` memory sizes are always the 64-bit ones.</b> libxm computes them with `sizeof` of
    pointer-holding structures; a 32-bit build (the web version is one) would declare too little and the
    player refuses the module. `Xm64Converter` always declares the 64-bit sizes.
-   <b>Run one `audioconv64` per module.</b> Over several XM files in one run it writes later modules with
    the first one's offsets, which hangs or crashes the console. The packer does this; do the same by hand.
-   <b>Identical samples keep only the first instrument's seek points in an `.xm64`.</b> Another instrument
    sharing the data and played from a different `9xx` offset asserts on the console ("invalid VADPCM
    seeking point"), so the packer makes such copies differ inaudibly
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`ModuleConverter.cpp`) @m_endspan.

*/

}
